\par\par \b \uc2 Global Equities Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 684 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2026\par 08:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering Equities. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0602 GMT - The Philippines' New Clark City is likely a focus for the property sector, \uc2 Maybank Securities\uc2 ' Raffy Mendoza says in a research report. The U.S.-led Pax Silica Initiative's proposed AI industrial hub is expected to span 1,619 hectares in this city, the analyst notes. The hub is generating strong interest from multi-national companies, with the brokerage seeing it as a potential demand multiplier for the property sector. Among property holdings of the brokerage's covered developers near this city, \uc2 Ayala Land\uc2  is the biggest potential beneficiary. The brokerage has a buy rating on \uc2 Ayala Land\uc2  with a target price of 35.00 pesos. Shares are 1.05% lower at 15.14 pesos. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0539 GMT - Supalai is likely to continue gaining share of Thailand's residential property market, TTB Wealth Securities' Rata Limsuthiwanpoom says in a research report. The company is one of Thailand's top three residential property developers increasing market share amid a flight to quality by buyers wary of developers with weak balance sheets. The company is known as a low-cost developer with a strong balance sheet, the analyst notes. However, the brokerage cuts its 2026-2028 earnings forecasts for Supalai by 15%-19% to reflect more conservative assumptions on demand and margins. It lowers the stock's target price to 18.00 baht from 20.50 baht, with an unchanged buy rating. Shares are 1.3% higher at 15.80 baht. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0513 GMT - Uncertainty over \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2 ' and \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2 's potential capital-expenditure is likely pressuring their share prices, says \uc2 Morningstar\uc2 's Jing Jie Yu in commentary. The two companies could be planning to spend around a combined 2 quadrillion won over the next decade, nearly double \uc2 Morningstar\uc2 's capex estimates. This could include \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2 's plans to invest in \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  data centers, with its Yongin fab cluster commitments already expected at 600 trillion won, he notes. Should \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2 's AI data center commitment and its potential capex plans be separate, the analyst flags material risk of oversupply for memory producers, given that demand tends to taper off during the tail years of capex cycles. \uc2 Samsung\uc2  shares fall 6.5% to 317,000 won, while \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2  shares drop 4.7% to 2,548,000 won. (megan.cheah@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0508 GMT - Eternal Precision Mechanics is likely benefiting from demand for Ajinomoto build-up film substrates, Daiwa Capital Markets analysts say in a research report. The Taiwanese company has around 95% share in the high-end vacuum laminator market, and direct exposure to the ABF substrate industry, the analysts note. Given the company's strong correlation with the industry's capital expenditure and increasing demand for high-end equipment driven by AI chip complexity, the brokerage forecasts the company's revenue CAGR at 96% over 2026-2028. Daiwa initiates coverage of the stock with a buy rating and a target price of 600.00 New Taiwan dollars. Shares are 9.9% higher at NT$506.00. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT - Investors should be selective and focus on Chinese consumer stocks with specific catalysts, given the overall sector's uneven recovery, say DBS Group Research analysts in a note. These catalysts include local-for-local strategies where companies move their supply chains and manufacture locally, which could help them mitigate tariff risks, they say. Companies with scale advantages on procurement and pricing power should also see stronger market-share gains, they add. Those within niche growth segments such as the small-ticket discretionary and functional categories could also see growth, DBS adds. The bank favors Midea, \uc2 Yum China\uc2  and \uc2 Anta Sports Products\uc2  due to their structural growth visibility. Meanwhile, it flags that companies with high 2H bases such as \uc2 Pop Mart\uc2  and \uc2 Sands China\uc2  could see slower growth momentum. (megan.cheah@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 29, 2026 02:02 ET (06:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260629em6t000az\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
