\par\par \b \uc2 Micron\uc2  Breaks the Cycle. Why the Stock Could Still Double From Here. -- Barrons.com\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Levine \par \uc2 977 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2026\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 It's better to be lucky than to be smart, but it's best to be both. That's where \uc2 Micron Technology\uc2  is today.\par \par \uc2 The memory-chip maker was in the right place at the right time, but it's also being smart about it, using its once-in-a-lifetime market power to possibly reshape the memory business in the long term. And might just get its stock a higher valuation in the process.\par \par \uc2 The memory business has long been commoditized, as Intel first discovered when Japanese competitors chased it out of the market in the 1980s. There are wild swings in inventories and prices, and sales growth and gross profit margin exhibit a highly cyclical pattern.\par \par \uc2 The result is that investor psychology around memory stocks more resembles that of oil traders than stock traders. As the old saw goes, the best time to get into memory stocks is when things go from bleak to merely awful, and the best time to get out is when gross margins are peaking. Many still bear the emotional scars from a time they mistimed the cycle; investors lack long-term conviction and always have one foot out the door.\par \par \uc2 As a consequence, memory companies like \uc2 Micron\uc2  trade at very low price/earnings multiples. Over the past 10 years, \uc2 Micron\uc2 's median P/E ratio for the next 12 months was about half that of the S&P 500 index.\par \par \uc2 The boom-and-bust pattern seemed to be playing out once again coming out of the pandemic. In fact, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  investment boom began in 2024, the memory companies were coming off one of the worst down cycles ever. Supply was bloated, prices crashed, and \uc2 Micron\uc2  had a negative gross margin for four consecutive quarters in 2023 as it racked up inventory charges.\par \par \uc2 It meant that \uc2 Micron\uc2  and its peers were in no mood to start another heavy capital expenditures cycle to build up the manufacturing capacity that the AI boom would need. \uc2 Micron\uc2 's decisions in 2024 are biting memory customers in 2026 and 2027. Today the industry is facing the biggest memory shortage ever with the highest price increases ever. Because of the long lead times these new factories require, no new major manufacturing capacity will begin to come on line until about a year from now, with more slated for 2028.\par \par \uc2 "Our customers are recognizing that supply shortages in memory and storage will take considerable time to improve," \uc2 Micron\uc2  CEO \uc2 Sanjay Mehrotra\uc2  said during the company's earnings call on Wednesday. "Even as we expect industry supply to improve gradually in 2028, we currently do not have line of sight as to when memory supply will be able to catch up with increasing demand."\par \par \uc2 Micron\uc2  has never before had this sort of market power, and it didn't take Mehrotra long to start throwing around his newfound muscle. \uc2 Micron\uc2  had an 85% gross margin in the third quarter, and expects it to rise to 86% in the current quarter -- that equates to a 600% markup on \uc2 Micron\uc2 's cost of goods.\par \par \uc2 "We view \uc2 Micron\uc2 's print as further evidence that the memory industry is undergoing a transformative shift towards long-term supply agreements which supports demand assurance, through-cycle profitability, and mitigated cyclicality," \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  analyst \uc2 Karl Ackerman\uc2  wrote to clients.\par \par \uc2 Micron\uc2  spent this year getting customers into three-to-five-year take-or-pay supply contracts with high price floors and ceilings and cash deposits. It already has 16 such agreements covering a range of customer sizes. Once all are executed, these favorable contracts will constitute about 40% of \uc2 Micron\uc2 's revenue, and the company hopes to raise that percentage.\par \par \uc2 The price floor will be "well above our peak quarterly margins in any past cycle," Mehrotra said, which was 61% in the fourth quarter of 2018. Since 2010, half of \uc2 Micron\uc2 's quarters had gross margins below 32%, so anything in the 60% range will prove immensely profitable -- and it's locked in through 2030 for a big portion of its customers.\par \par \uc2 Companies may be compelled to enter into these contracts to guarantee their access to memory and storage chips, because of their crucial role in all kinds of products from AI servers to smartphones to cars. The second factor is companies trying to hedge against future price increases on memory.\par \par \uc2 Micron\uc2  is hoping to smooth out its down cycles and give investors more long-term confidence in the stock. "We are putting a lot of effort into not only doing these [long-term agreements] but communicating a lot of the details, and it'll take investors some time to understand all the details," \uc2 Micron\uc2  Chief Business Officer \uc2 Sumit Sadana\uc2  told Barron's.\par \par \uc2 Maybe \uc2 Micron\uc2  can even convince investors that the company is worth more than nine times forward earnings, and value it to something closer to the S&P 500. Such a rerating would mean another 100% upside for the stock, with no increase in expected earnings.\par \par \uc2 But in fact, expectations are increasing, in which case even a doubling of the stock may be too conservative. In the wake of \uc2 Micron\uc2 's earnings this week, 35 out of 42 analysts tracked by FactSet raised their earnings forecasts dramatically. The consensus earnings-per-share estimate for the next fiscal year now stands at $144.27, up from $101.74 last month.\par \par \uc2 Few companies see their earnings power change so quickly. For now, investors are still catching up.\par \par \uc2 Write to Adam Levine at adam.levine@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 This content was created by Barron's, which is operated by Dow Jones & Co. Barron's is published independently from Dow Jones Newswires and The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 26, 2026 01:00 ET (05:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260626em6q00084\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
