\par\par \b \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  Stock Is Having Its Worst Start to a Year Since the Dot-Com Crash -- Barrons.com\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mackenzie Tatananni \par \uc2 582 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2026\par 19:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 There's no end in sight to \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's historic stock slump.\par \par \uc2 Shares are pacing towards their worst start to a year since the dot-com crash. Heading into Thursday's session, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  stock had fallen more than 24% in 2026, putting it on track for its steepest first-half decline since a nearly 32% drop in 2000, according to Dow Jones Market Data.\par \par \uc2 For much of its history, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  was best known as the pioneer behind the Windows operating system and wildly popular Office software. But as the company increasingly embraced \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  along with the rest of the tech industry, this new focus has created problems.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  seemed like an obvious early winner in AI: The company became \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's first major corporate backer through a $1 billion investment in 2019, aligning itself early on with one of the industry's juggernauts. At the same time, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  moved aggressively to embed the technology across its own cloud and software businesses.\par \par \uc2 But issues quickly arose. First came the pushback from users themselves, who perceived the integration of AI features -- particularly \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's Copilot assistant -- as forced. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  has since softened its approach, dialing back the branding of some AI tools and even discontinuing free, built-in Copilot access in certain Office applications.\par \par \uc2 Lately, attention has centered on the massive capital expenditures driving \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's AI push. It isn't the only company under pressure -- sector peers \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  and \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 -owned \uc2 Google\uc2  have faced similar scrutiny from investors.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and other hyperscalers increasingly issue debt to support their spending instead of using free cash flow, investor concerns are beginning to weigh on their stock prices. Even though \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's latest quarterly print signaled growing traction for Azure, its AI-forward cloud platform, this detail was overshadowed by lofty capex guidance that drove shares down nearly 4% in the wake of the report.\par \par \uc2 The stock fell another 3.7% on Thursday as a broad tech selloff resumed, fueled by fears of heavy AI spending. Micron's fiscal third-quarter earnings, released after the bell Wednesday, offered only a temporary reprieve that failed to stop the slide.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  has doubled down on its AI strategy despite these clear anxieties. Earlier this month, the company unveiled its own in-house foundational models to put more distance between itself and \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 . The companies remain partners, though the nature of their relationship has shifted over time; in April, they jointly announced that \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  would be allowed to "serve all its products to customers across any cloud provider," rather than just Azure.\par \par \uc2 The costs of powering AI processors, generating electricity, and building data centers continue to swell. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's capex has steadily ratcheted higher, climbing from $24 billion in fiscal 2021 to $88 billion in 2025. The company anticipates spending will balloon even further in 2026 to a projected $190 billion.\par \par \uc2 It's clear \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  has no intention of retreating, even as the market signals its exhaustion. But until these initiatives translate into sufficient profits, the tech giant's historic market hangover is unlikely to clear.\par \par \uc2 Write to Mackenzie Tatananni at mackenzie.tatananni@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 